WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-TR

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.110
  

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.159
How the economy machine works in 30 minutes.  The

00:00:03.159 --> 00:00:06.680
economy works like a simple machine.

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:09.390
But many people don't understand it

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:11.490
– or disagree about how it works

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:15.150
– and this has led to a lot of economic hardship.

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:18.249
I feel a deep sense of responsibility to share my simple yet practical economic model

00:00:18.249 --> 00:00:22.049
.

00:00:23.049 --> 00:00:25.320
Although unusual, this

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:30.150
helped me predict and avoid this global financial crisis,

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:32.460
and it has served me well for the past 30 years.  Let's

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:34.260
begin.

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:39.520
The economy may seem complex, but...  It works in a simple, mechanical way.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:43.930
It consists of a few simple parts and simple operations.

00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:47.260
This is repeated millions of times.

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:51.460
These processes are primarily driven by human nature

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:54.800
and constitute the three main forces that drive the economy.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:58.120
One: Productivity growth;

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:01.650
Two: Short-term debt cycle;

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:04.400
and Three: Long-term debt cycle.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:07.770
We will look at these three forces and examine how, when superimposed, they form a good model for

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:11.530
tracking economic movements and understanding what is happening now

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:13.590
.

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:16.830
Let's start with the simplest part of economics:

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:18.860
transactions.

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:23.840
An economy is simply the sum of the transactions that comprise it,

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:26.570
and a transaction is a very simple thing.

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:28.360
You perform these actions constantly.

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:31.760
Every time you buy something, you're making a transaction.

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:35.240
Every transaction consists of

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:37.150
a buyer exchanging money or credit

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:42.550
and a seller of goods, services, or financial assets.

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:45.150
Credit is spent just like money.

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:49.280
Therefore, by adding up the amount of money spent and the amount of credit used,

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:52.160
you know the total expenditure.

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:55.560
Total spending drives the economy.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:57.590
If

00:01:57.590 --> 00:01:59.990
you divide the total cost by the amount sold,

00:01:59.990 --> 00:02:01.290
you'll find the price.

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:04.380
And that's it.  This is a process.  This is the

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:07.110
building block of this economic machine.

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:12.019
In an economy, all cycles and all forces are driven by transactions.

00:02:12.019 --> 00:02:14.469
So, if we understand the processes,

00:02:14.469 --> 00:02:16.569
we can understand the entire economy.

00:02:16.569 --> 00:02:19.510
A market consists of all buyers

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:20.510
and all sellers

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:22.910
transacting for the same thing.

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:25.540
For example, there's a wheat market,

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:26.840
a car market,

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:27.640
a stock exchange,

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:29.550
and markets for millions of other things.

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:33.470
An economy

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:34.670
includes all the transactions in these markets.

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:37.550
If you add up the

00:02:37.550 --> 00:02:39.450
total spending and total sales amounts in all these markets

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:40.430
;  You will get everything you need to know

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:42.030
to understand the economy

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:43.630
.  It's that

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:45.960
simple.

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:49.430
People, businesses, banks, and governments

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:53.730
all conduct business in the way I've described:

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:58.530
exchanging money and credit for goods, services, and financial assets.

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:01.500
The government, being the largest buyer and seller,

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:04.040
consists of two main branches:

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:07.690
a Central Administration that collects taxes and spends money,

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:09.669
and a Central Bank.  The

00:03:09.669 --> 00:03:12.269
central bank differs from other buyers and sellers

00:03:12.269 --> 00:03:15.989
because it controls the amount of money and credit in the economy.  It does

00:03:16.689 --> 00:03:19.650
this by influencing interest rates

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:21.020
and printing new money.

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:23.620
For these reasons, we will see that the

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:27.200
Central Bank is a significant player in the flow of credit

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:28.700
.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:31.629
I want you to pay attention to the loan issue.

00:03:31.629 --> 00:03:34.659
Credit is the most important

00:03:34.659 --> 00:03:36.539
and probably the least understood part of the economy.  It's the

00:03:37.039 --> 00:03:40.319
most important part because it's the largest

00:03:40.319 --> 00:03:42.100
and most mobile component.  Just

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:45.100
as buyers and sellers go to the market,

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:49.969
lenders and borrowers also conduct transactions.

00:03:49.969 --> 00:03:53.569
Lenders generally want to make more money,

00:03:53.569 --> 00:03:57.260
and borrowers generally don't want to buy something they can't afford.  They might want to invest in something like

00:03:57.260 --> 00:03:59.059
a house or a car,

00:03:59.059 --> 00:04:02.319
or start a business.

00:04:03.019 --> 00:04:04.599
Credit

00:04:04.599 --> 00:04:07.799
can help both lenders and borrowers get what they want.

00:04:08.099 --> 00:04:11.560
Borrowers promise to repay the amount they borrowed, called the principal,

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:12.719
plus an additional amount called interest

00:04:12.719 --> 00:04:15.419
.

00:04:15.419 --> 00:04:17.560
When interest rates are high, people

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:20.560
borrow less;  Because it is very expensive.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:22.540
When interest rates are low,

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:25.040
borrowing increases;  Because it's cheap.  Lenders believe borrowers

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:27.930
when they promise to repay

00:04:28.330 --> 00:04:29.959
.  A

00:04:29.959 --> 00:04:31.059
loan is created.

00:04:31.659 --> 00:04:35.170
Any two people can agree to create a loan out of nothing!

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:39.210
It seems simple enough, but credit is deceptive

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:40.499
because there are different names involved.

00:04:40.499 --> 00:04:43.599
The moment a loan is created,

00:04:43.599 --> 00:04:45.029
it immediately turns into debt.

00:04:46.229 --> 00:04:48.670
Debt is an asset belonging to both the lender and the borrower,

00:04:48.670 --> 00:04:50.770
and it is a liability for the borrower.

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:52.540
In the future,

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:56.240
when the borrower pays the loan interest, the

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:58.549
property and liability are lost.

00:04:58.549 --> 00:05:00.949
And the transaction takes place.

00:05:01.749 --> 00:05:04.629
So why is credit so important?

00:05:05.229 --> 00:05:07.619
Because when a borrower takes out a loan, it's

00:05:07.619 --> 00:05:09.719
possible for them to increase their spending.

00:05:09.719 --> 00:05:13.349
And if you remember, spending stimulates the economy.  Because

00:05:13.349 --> 00:05:15.699
one person's spending is

00:05:15.699 --> 00:05:17.099
another person's income;

00:05:17.499 --> 00:05:22.189
Think of every dollar you spend as having been earned by someone else.

00:05:22.189 --> 00:05:25.279
And every dollar you earn goes to someone else.  So when

00:05:25.279 --> 00:05:29.149
we spend more, someone else earns more.

00:05:29.149 --> 00:05:32.649
When someone's income increases

00:05:32.649 --> 00:05:34.649
, lenders become more willing to lend to them

00:05:34.649 --> 00:05:37.629
because they are now seen as more deserving of credit.

00:05:37.629 --> 00:05:40.929
Being deemed worthy of a loan depends on two things.

00:05:40.929 --> 00:05:43.519
For repayment capacity and collateral.

00:05:44.319 --> 00:05:48.319
Having a substantial income provides the ability to repay debt.

00:05:49.079 --> 00:05:55.749
If the debtor is unable to repay, the income can be used as collateral.  It owns valuable properties that can be sold.

00:05:55.749 --> 00:06:00.340
This makes the lender feel more comfortable giving money.

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:03.960
In other words, income increase;  Borrowing is permitted,

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:05.680
which leads to increased spending.

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:09.920
And since one person's spending is another person's income,

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:13.360
this leads to even greater debt.

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:17.350
These self-reinforcing events lead to economic growth;

00:06:17.350 --> 00:06:21.640
therefore, fluctuations occur.

00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:25.620
In a transaction, you have to give something to receive something,

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:28.830
and how much you receive depends on how much you produce.

00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:31.430
Over time, we received education,

00:06:31.430 --> 00:06:34.680
and this accumulated knowledge improved our living standards.  We

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:36.930
call this increased productivity.

00:06:37.430 --> 00:06:40.430
Those who were creative and hardworking increased

00:06:40.430 --> 00:06:43.540
their productivity and living standards faster than those who were indifferent and lazy

00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:46.950
.

00:06:48.850 --> 00:06:49.710
But this isn't very important in the short term.

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:54.340
Productivity is important in the long run, but credit is most important in the short run.

00:06:54.340 --> 00:06:58.370
Because productivity increases don't fluctuate much, and

00:06:58.370 --> 00:07:01.480
productivity isn't the main cause of economic fluctuations.

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:06.750
The cause is debt – it allows us to consume more than we produce when we are in debt,

00:07:06.750 --> 00:07:11.050
and forces us to consume less than we produce when we are paying off the debt.

00:07:11.050 --> 00:07:14.770
Debt fluctuations occur in two major cycles.

00:07:14.770 --> 00:07:20.830
One lasts about 5 to 8 years, and the other lasts about 75 to 100 years.

00:07:20.830 --> 00:07:25.160
While most people feel the fluctuations, they don't perceive it as a cycle

00:07:25.160 --> 00:07:29.490
because they see the waves so closely – day by day, week by week. In

00:07:29.490 --> 00:07:32.950
this section, to understand these three great forces, we will take a step back and

00:07:32.950 --> 00:07:37.000
look at how they affect our lives together.

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:42.340
As mentioned, the wave around the right path depends less on how much innovation or hard work there is, and

00:07:42.340 --> 00:07:46.240
more on how much credit there is.

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:50.070
Let's imagine for a second an economy without credit.

00:07:50.070 --> 00:07:53.810
In this economy, the only way to increase my spending is

00:07:53.810 --> 00:07:55.480
to increase my income.

00:07:55.480 --> 00:07:59.080
This requires me to be more efficient and work harder.

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:03.020
Increased productivity is the only way to grow.

00:08:03.020 --> 00:08:05.250
Since my spending is another person's income, the

00:08:05.250 --> 00:08:10.430
economy grows whenever I or someone else becomes more productive.  If

00:08:10.430 --> 00:08:13.530
we follow the processes and look at the whole picture,

00:08:13.530 --> 00:08:16.910
we see a process similar to a productivity increase curve.

00:08:16.910 --> 00:08:20.780
Because we've borrowed money, there will be fluctuations.

00:08:20.780 --> 00:08:23.900
This happens not because of any law or regulation, but

00:08:23.900 --> 00:08:27.660
because of human nature and credit.

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:32.270
Think of borrowing simply as an action to increase spending.  To

00:08:32.270 --> 00:08:37.479
buy something you can't afford, you have to spend more than you earn.

00:08:37.479 --> 00:08:41.460
To do this, you essentially need to borrow from your future self.

00:08:41.460 --> 00:08:44.610
By doing this,

00:08:44.610 --> 00:08:48.690
you create a cycle where you spend less than you earn when you repay in the future.

00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:51.950
This is exactly like a cycle.

00:08:51.950 --> 00:08:55.400
Essentially, you create a cycle whenever you take out a loan.

00:08:55.400 --> 00:08:59.950
This applies to the economy as well as to an individual.

00:08:59.950 --> 00:09:01.850
Understanding credit is therefore important

00:09:01.850 --> 00:09:04.590
because credit transactions

00:09:04.590 --> 00:09:08.790
trigger a series of mechanical and predictable events that will occur in the future.

00:09:08.790 --> 00:09:11.570
This separates money from credit.

00:09:13.470 --> 00:09:14.580
It's what you use to conduct your financial transactions.

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:18.120
When you buy a beer from a bartender with cash, the

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:20.800
transaction happens instantly.

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:22.630
But if you buy a beer on credit, it's

00:09:22.630 --> 00:09:24.730
similar to getting a receipt.  You say you

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:27.530
promised to pay later.

00:09:27.530 --> 00:09:32.070
Together, you and the bartender create a credit and a payable.

00:09:32.070 --> 00:09:35.250
You just created a loan.  Out of nowhere!

00:09:35.250 --> 00:09:38.900


00:09:38.900 --> 00:09:41.250
The debt and credit will not disappear until the bill is paid;

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:42.550


00:09:42.650 --> 00:09:44.090
The transaction will be completed once the debt is paid off.

00:09:45.090 --> 00:09:50.250
In reality, what people call money is often credit.  The

00:09:50.250 --> 00:09:55.510
total amount of credit in the United States is approximately $50 trillion,

00:09:55.510 --> 00:09:59.680
and the total amount of money is only about $3 trillion.

00:09:59.680 --> 00:10:02.040
Remember, in an economy without credit: the

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:05.200
only way to increase spending was to produce more.

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:07.280
But in a credit-based economy,

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:10.780
you can also increase your spending through borrowing.

00:10:10.780 --> 00:10:14.120
In conclusion, there is more spending in a credit-based economy, which

00:10:14.120 --> 00:10:18.090
causes incomes to rise faster than productivity in the short term,

00:10:18.090 --> 00:10:19.890
but the opposite happens in the long term.

00:10:20.090 --> 00:10:21.360
Now, don't get me wrong,

00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:25.660
credit isn't necessarily a bad thing that causes cycles.

00:10:25.660 --> 00:10:30.900
Financing excessive consumption that cannot be repaid is bad.

00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:34.490
However, credit is a good thing when it increases the efficient use of resources

00:10:34.490 --> 00:10:37.690
and allows you to increase your income and repay the debt.

00:10:37.690 --> 00:10:40.980
For example, if you take out a loan to buy a large TV,

00:10:40.980 --> 00:10:43.879
it won't ensure your income increases while you're paying off the loan.

00:10:44.079 --> 00:10:47.880
But, if you take out a loan to buy a tractor—

00:10:48.380 --> 00:10:51.560
and that tractor leads to you harvesting more crops and earning more money—

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:53.090
then you can repay the loan

00:10:53.090 --> 00:10:56.490
and improve your standard of living.

00:10:56.490 --> 00:10:57.600
In a credit-based economy,

00:10:57.600 --> 00:10:59.600
we track transactions

00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:01.300
and see how credit fuels economic growth.  Let me

00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:03.680
give you an example:

00:11:04.080 --> 00:11:08.190
suppose you earn $100,000 annually and have no debts.

00:11:08.390 --> 00:11:11.540
You have sufficient creditworthiness to qualify for a $10,000 loan.

00:11:11.540 --> 00:11:13.040
- Let's say it's on this credit card

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:15.250
- So even if you're only earning $100,000,

00:11:15.250 --> 00:11:18.250
you can spend $110,000.

00:11:18.750 --> 00:11:21.460
Since your expenditure is someone else's income,

00:11:21.460 --> 00:11:24.460
someone would have earned $110,000.  A

00:11:25.060 --> 00:11:27.670
person earning $110,000 with no debt can

00:11:27.770 --> 00:11:31.429
then borrow $11,000, allowing them to spend $121,000

00:11:31.429 --> 00:11:34.070
despite only earning $110,000

00:11:34.070 --> 00:11:38.170
.

00:11:38.170 --> 00:11:40.640
His spending is also someone else's income,

00:11:40.640 --> 00:11:43.430
and if we track the transactions,

00:11:43.430 --> 00:11:45.430


00:11:45.430 --> 00:11:47.430
we can begin to see how this self-reinforcing process works through repetition.

00:11:47.830 --> 00:11:51.600
But remember;  It creates debt cycles,

00:11:51.600 --> 00:11:55.809
and if the cycle goes up, it eventually has to come down.

00:11:56.009 --> 00:11:59.710
This leads us to a short-term debt cycle.

00:12:00.810 --> 00:12:04.380
As economic activity increases, we see an expansion, which is the

00:12:04.380 --> 00:12:06.840
first phase of the short-term debt cycle.

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:10.720
Spending continues to increase, and prices begin to rise.

00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:15.230
This is because the increase in spending is fueled by credit.

00:12:15.230 --> 00:12:17.450
- Credit is also something that can be created out of nothing.

00:12:17.450 --> 00:12:22.680
Whenever the amount of spending and income grows faster than the production of goods:

00:12:22.680 --> 00:12:23.880
prices rise.

00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:28.000
When prices rise, we call it inflation.  The

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:32.500
central bank doesn't want too much inflation

00:12:32.500 --> 00:12:35.010
because it causes problems.

00:12:35.010 --> 00:12:38.250
When it sees prices rising, it raises interest rates.

00:12:38.250 --> 00:12:42.959
With high interest rates, fewer people can afford to borrow money.

00:12:42.959 --> 00:12:45.600
And the cost of existing debt rises.

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:50.100
Think of it like your monthly credit card payments increasing.

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:54.459
Because people borrow less and have higher debt repayments, they have

00:12:54.459 --> 00:12:58.720
less money left to spend, so spending slows down

00:12:58.720 --> 00:13:02.779
... and since one person's spending is another person's income,

00:13:02.779 --> 00:13:06.589
incomes fall one after another.

00:13:06.589 --> 00:13:10.800
When people spend less, prices fall.

00:13:10.800 --> 00:13:12.689
We call this deflation.

00:13:12.689 --> 00:13:17.460
Economic activity decreases and a recession occurs.

00:13:17.460 --> 00:13:20.060
If the recession becomes very severe

00:13:20.060 --> 00:13:22.440
and inflation is no longer a problem, the

00:13:22.440 --> 00:13:27.060
central bank will lower interest rates again to stimulate recovery.

00:13:27.060 --> 00:13:29.650
With low interest rates,

00:13:29.650 --> 00:13:30.650
debt repayments decrease,

00:13:30.650 --> 00:13:33.250
borrowing and spending recover,

00:13:33.250 --> 00:13:35.450
and we see another expansion.

00:13:35.450 --> 00:13:39.450
As you can see, the economy works like a machine.

00:13:40.250 --> 00:13:44.160
In a short-term debt cycle, spending

00:13:44.160 --> 00:13:47.140
is limited only by the willingness of the lender and borrower to lend and receive credit.

00:13:47.140 --> 00:13:52.510
When credit becomes readily available, economic expansion occurs.

00:13:52.510 --> 00:13:56.090
When credit is not readily available, a recession occurs.

00:13:56.090 --> 00:14:00.450
Remember that this cycle is primarily managed by the central bank.

00:14:00.450 --> 00:14:05.120
Short-term debt cycles typically last 5-8 years

00:14:05.120 --> 00:14:08.410
and are repeated over and over again.

00:14:08.410 --> 00:14:10.410
But note that each period

00:14:10.410 --> 00:14:12.400


00:14:12.400 --> 00:14:16.670
ends with both higher and lower levels of growth and debt than the previous period.

00:14:16.670 --> 00:14:18.040
From where?

00:14:18.040 --> 00:14:20.740
Because people push their limits

00:14:20.740 --> 00:14:25.530
– and people tend to borrow and spend more rather than repay their debts.

00:14:25.530 --> 00:14:27.480
That's human nature.

00:14:27.480 --> 00:14:29.380
Therefore,

00:14:29.380 --> 00:14:30.680
over a long period,

00:14:30.680 --> 00:14:33.470
debts rise faster than income,

00:14:33.470 --> 00:14:36.670
creating a Long-Term Debt Cycle.

00:14:38.070 --> 00:14:39.670
Even though people are becoming more indebted,

00:14:40.170 --> 00:14:44.030
lenders are making it even easier to hand out loans.

00:14:44.030 --> 00:14:45.030
From where?

00:14:45.030 --> 00:14:48.670
Because everyone thinks things are going great!

00:14:48.670 --> 00:14:51.970
People only focus on recent events.

00:14:51.970 --> 00:14:55.650
And what's been happening lately?

00:14:55.650 --> 00:14:57.650
Revenues are on the rise!

00:14:57.650 --> 00:14:59.250
The value of their securities is increasing!  The

00:14:59.250 --> 00:15:01.550
stock market is roaring!

00:15:01.550 --> 00:15:02.480
This is an explosion!

00:15:02.480 --> 00:15:06.690
Goods, services, and financial assets

00:15:06.690 --> 00:15:08.390
are being purchased with borrowed money!  When

00:15:08.390 --> 00:15:11.770
people do this repeatedly, we call it a bubble.

00:15:11.770 --> 00:15:14.640
So, even though debts are increasing,

00:15:14.640 --> 00:15:18.110
revenues are also increasing fast enough to offset them.

00:15:18.110 --> 00:15:21.970
Let's call the ratio of debt to income the debt burden.

00:15:21.970 --> 00:15:25.210
As revenues continue to rise, the

00:15:25.210 --> 00:15:27.040
debt burden remains manageable.  At

00:15:27.040 --> 00:15:30.230
the same time, property values ​​multiply.

00:15:30.230 --> 00:15:33.230
People

00:15:34.030 --> 00:15:35.060
borrow large amounts of money to buy property as investments.

00:15:35.060 --> 00:15:37.660
This will cause prices to rise even further.

00:15:37.660 --> 00:15:40.260
People feel rich.

00:15:40.260 --> 00:15:43.260
Therefore, despite excessive debt accumulation,

00:15:43.260 --> 00:15:49.490
rising incomes and property values ​​help borrowers remain worthy of lending for a long time.

00:15:49.490 --> 00:15:52.530
But this certainly can't go on forever.

00:15:52.530 --> 00:15:54.250
And it wouldn't, anyway.

00:15:54.250 --> 00:16:00.520
Over the decades, the debt burden gradually increases, creating larger debt repayments.

00:16:00.520 --> 00:16:05.400
At some point, debt repayments begin to grow faster than income,

00:16:05.400 --> 00:16:08.240
forcing people to cut back on spending.

00:16:08.240 --> 00:16:12.200
And since one person's spending is another person's income,

00:16:12.200 --> 00:16:14.500
incomes start to fall...

00:16:14.500 --> 00:16:19.020
People's credibility decreases, borrowing decreases.

00:16:19.020 --> 00:16:22.020
Debt repayments continue to increase,

00:16:22.020 --> 00:16:24.440
leading to further reductions in spending...

00:16:24.440 --> 00:16:27.140
...And the cycle reverses.

00:16:27.140 --> 00:16:30.960
This is the peak of long-term debt.  The

00:16:30.960 --> 00:16:34.150
debt burden has grown very large.  This is what happened in 2008

00:16:34.150 --> 00:16:38.830
for the United States, Europe, and the rest of the world

00:16:38.830 --> 00:16:40.760
.  The same thing happened

00:16:40.760 --> 00:16:45.430
in Japan in 1989,

00:16:45.430 --> 00:16:48.750
and again in the United States in 1929.

00:16:48.750 --> 00:16:51.980
Now the balloon will start to deflate.

00:16:51.980 --> 00:16:56.500
With the decrease in borrowing rates;  People cut back on spending,

00:16:56.500 --> 00:16:59.540
incomes fall, credit disappears,

00:16:59.540 --> 00:17:02.700
properties become cheaper, banks struggle, the

00:17:02.700 --> 00:17:06.340
stock market crashes, social tensions increase,

00:17:06.340 --> 00:17:09.709
and the entire system begins to collapse.  As

00:17:09.709 --> 00:17:13.850
incomes fall and debt repayments increase,

00:17:13.850 --> 00:17:17.990
borrowers begin to struggle.  They are no longer worth lending to;

00:17:17.990 --> 00:17:22.360
credit lines dry up, and borrowers

00:17:22.360 --> 00:17:23.600
cannot obtain loans to make debt repayments.

00:17:23.600 --> 00:17:28.440
To fill this gap, debtors are forced to sell their properties.

00:17:28.440 --> 00:17:31.550
They rush to the market to sell their properties.

00:17:31.550 --> 00:17:36.370
When the stock market crashes, the

00:17:36.370 --> 00:17:39.740
real estate market collapses and banks get into trouble.  As

00:17:39.740 --> 00:17:44.940
property prices fall, the value of the collateral borrowers use to secure loans also decreases.

00:17:44.940 --> 00:17:48.280
This makes borrowers less attractive for lending.

00:17:48.280 --> 00:17:50.200
People feel poor.

00:17:51.300 --> 00:17:55.010
Credit disappears quickly.  Less spending &gt;

00:17:55.010 --> 00:17:55.890
less income &gt;

00:17:55.890 --> 00:17:57.890
less wealth &gt;

00:17:57.890 --> 00:17:58.590
less credit &gt;

00:17:58.590 --> 00:18:00.800
less debt, and so on.

00:18:00.800 --> 00:18:03.000
This is a vicious cycle.

00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:06.530
Although it resembles a recession, there is a difference here.

00:18:06.530 --> 00:18:10.160
Lowering interest rates won't solve the immediate problem.

00:18:10.160 --> 00:18:14.590
During a recession, lowering interest rates encourages borrowing.

00:18:14.590 --> 00:18:18.570
However, when the economy shrinks like this, lowering interest rates is ineffective

00:18:18.570 --> 00:18:20.530
because interest rates

00:18:20.530 --> 00:18:25.310
are already low and will soon fall to 0% – meaning stimulus is not working.

00:18:25.310 --> 00:18:29.210
Interest rates in the United States hit 0% in the 1930s

00:18:29.210 --> 00:18:30.840
and again in 2008

00:18:30.840 --> 00:18:33.440
.

00:18:33.440 --> 00:18:35.840
The difference between a recession and an economic contraction is that

00:18:35.840 --> 00:18:40.270
in a contraction, the borrower's debt burden

00:18:40.270 --> 00:18:41.930
has become very large, and

00:18:41.930 --> 00:18:45.050
the situation cannot be corrected by lowering interest rates.

00:18:45.050 --> 00:18:50.240
Creditors realize the debts are too large to be fully repaid.

00:18:50.240 --> 00:18:55.310
Debtors lose their ability to repay and their collateral loses its value.

00:18:55.310 --> 00:18:59.370
They feel ruined by debt – they don't even want to borrow anymore!

00:18:59.370 --> 00:19:03.490
Lenders will stop lending.  Borrowers stop borrowing.  -Imagine

00:19:03.490 --> 00:19:07.020
the economy not being able to lend like an individual, or having any

00:19:07.020 --> 00:19:09.000
value.

00:19:09.000 --> 00:19:12.600
What should you do in this situation?

00:19:12.600 --> 00:19:17.540
The problem is that the debt burden is too high, and it needs to be reduced.

00:19:17.540 --> 00:19:20.790
There are four ways to do this.

00:19:20.790 --> 00:19:24.489
One: individuals, businesses, and governments cut spending.

00:19:24.489 --> 00:19:28.409
Two: debts are reduced through default and restructuring activities.

00:19:28.409 --> 00:19:34.139
Third: wealth is distributed from the 'rich' to the 'poor'.

00:19:34.139 --> 00:19:37.980
And finally, fourth: the central bank prints new money.

00:19:37.980 --> 00:19:42.999
These four strategies have been applied during every economic crisis in modern history.

00:19:45.699 --> 00:19:47.779
Typically, the initial expenses are cut.

00:19:47.879 --> 00:19:52.129
As we've seen, individuals, businesses, banks, and even governments

00:19:52.129 --> 00:19:54.869
are tightening their belts to cut spending and pay off debts.

00:19:54.869 --> 00:19:58.559
This is often referred to as saving money.  When

00:19:58.559 --> 00:20:01.830
borrowers stop taking on new debt

00:20:01.830 --> 00:20:06.769
and start paying off their old debts, you can expect your debt burden to decrease.

00:20:06.769 --> 00:20:10.720
But the opposite happens!  Because spending is cut

00:20:10.720 --> 00:20:14.610
– and one man's spending is another man's income – this

00:20:14.610 --> 00:20:19.029
leads to a decrease in income.  Revenues fall faster than debt repayments,

00:20:19.029 --> 00:20:23.210
and the debt burden actually worsens.  As we've seen,

00:20:23.210 --> 00:20:26.330
cutting spending is deflationary and painful.

00:20:26.330 --> 00:20:29.389
Businesses are forced to reduce their costs...

00:20:29.389 --> 00:20:32.779
which means fewer jobs and higher unemployment.

00:20:32.779 --> 00:20:37.139
This leads to the next step: debts must be reduced!

00:20:37.139 --> 00:20:41.149
Many borrowers find themselves unable to pay their loans

00:20:41.149 --> 00:20:44.299
– and a borrower's debts are a lender's receivables.  When

00:20:44.299 --> 00:20:48.749
debtors can't pay the bank, people worry the bank won't

00:20:48.749 --> 00:20:50.309
be able to pay their money,

00:20:50.309 --> 00:20:54.909
so they rush to withdraw their funds.  Banks run out of money;

00:20:54.909 --> 00:20:55.590
people,

00:20:55.590 --> 00:21:00.080
businesses, and banks default.  This severe

00:21:00.080 --> 00:21:03.570
economic contraction is a depression.

00:21:03.570 --> 00:21:08.649
A large part of depression is people

00:21:08.649 --> 00:21:11.089
discovering that the wealth they thought they possessed doesn't actually exist. Let's

00:21:11.089 --> 00:21:13.490
go back to the bar.  When

00:21:13.690 --> 00:21:17.619
you ordered a beer and the bill arrived,

00:21:17.619 --> 00:21:23.570
you promised the bartender you would pay.  Your word has become a debt owed to the bartender.

00:21:23.570 --> 00:21:28.129
But if you don't keep your word – you don't pay him back and you actually

00:21:28.129 --> 00:21:29.309
default –

00:21:29.309 --> 00:21:32.649
then the bartender's money becomes worthless.

00:21:32.649 --> 00:21:35.779
In fact, the receivable disappears.

00:21:35.779 --> 00:21:39.710
Many lenders do not want to lose their receivables and

00:21:39.710 --> 00:21:40.759
agree to debt restructuring.

00:21:40.759 --> 00:21:44.320
Debt restructuring means making fewer payments, repaying

00:21:44.320 --> 00:21:48.049
over a longer period, or paying at

00:21:48.049 --> 00:21:52.330
a lower interest rate.

00:21:52.330 --> 00:21:56.889
The contract has been broken in a way that reduces the debt.  Creditors

00:21:56.889 --> 00:21:59.419
prefer a little to nothing.

00:21:59.419 --> 00:22:03.450
Even if the debt disappears, restructuring

00:22:03.450 --> 00:22:06.559
causes income and property values ​​to fall even faster,

00:22:06.559 --> 00:22:09.679
thus worsening the debt burden.

00:22:09.679 --> 00:22:12.950
Like cutting spending, debt reduction

00:22:12.950 --> 00:22:16.009
can be painful and deflationary.

00:22:16.009 --> 00:22:21.940
All of these effects impact the central government because lower revenues and less employment

00:22:21.940 --> 00:22:27.049
mean less tax revenue for the government.  At

00:22:27.049 --> 00:22:30.210
the same time, due to the increase in unemployment, it is necessary to increase government spending.

00:22:30.210 --> 00:22:33.960
Unemployed people cannot save money

00:22:33.960 --> 00:22:36.419
and request financial assistance from the government.

00:22:36.419 --> 00:22:39.999
In addition, governments create stimulus plans

00:22:39.999 --> 00:22:43.879
and increase their own spending to offset the economic downturn.

00:22:43.879 --> 00:22:47.509
Government budget deficits can explode during times of crisis

00:22:47.509 --> 00:22:51.059
because they spend more than they earn in taxes.

00:22:51.059 --> 00:22:55.720
This is what happens when you hear the budget deficit being discussed in the news.

00:22:55.720 --> 00:23:01.320
To close their deficits, governments need to either raise taxes or

00:23:01.320 --> 00:23:06.059
borrow money.  But where will the money come from when incomes are falling and unemployment is widespread

00:23:06.059 --> 00:23:09.789
?  The rich.  Because

00:23:09.789 --> 00:23:14.580
governments need more money and wealth

00:23:14.580 --> 00:23:17.109
is concentrated in a small segment of the population, they

00:23:17.109 --> 00:23:20.379
naturally raise taxes on the rich. This

00:23:20.379 --> 00:23:24.450
leads to a redistribution of wealth in the economy –

00:23:24.450 --> 00:23:29.279
from the 'rich' to the 'poor'.  The poor

00:23:29.279 --> 00:23:30.970
begin to resent the rich.  The

00:23:30.970 --> 00:23:36.309
wealthy 'well-off' are squeezed by a weak economy, falling prices, and high taxes, and they

00:23:36.309 --> 00:23:39.940
begin to resent the poor.

00:23:39.940 --> 00:23:43.890
If depression persists, social unrest may erupt.

00:23:43.890 --> 00:23:47.280
Not only domestic tensions but

00:23:47.280 --> 00:23:52.200
also tensions between countries can arise – especially between lenders and borrowers –

00:23:52.200 --> 00:23:56.040
This situation can lead to political change, and

00:23:56.040 --> 00:23:58.840
sometimes this change can go too far.

00:23:58.840 --> 00:24:02.630
In the 1930s, this led to Hitler's rise to power,

00:24:02.630 --> 00:24:08.080
war in Europe, and the Depression in the United States.  The pressure to end depression

00:24:08.080 --> 00:24:10.070
increases.

00:24:10.070 --> 00:24:14.950
Remember, much of what people think is money is actually credit.

00:24:14.950 --> 00:24:18.400
So, when credit disappears, people don't have enough money left.

00:24:18.400 --> 00:24:23.190
People desperately search for money, and do you remember who prints it?  The

00:24:23.190 --> 00:24:27.370
central bank can print it.  With

00:24:27.370 --> 00:24:30.309
interest rates already almost zero,

00:24:30.309 --> 00:24:34.090
they are forced to print money.  Unlike cutting spending,

00:24:34.090 --> 00:24:37.299
reducing debt, and redistributing wealth,

00:24:37.299 --> 00:24:42.190
monetary printing is inflationary and stimulating.  Inevitably, the central bank prints new money—

00:24:42.190 --> 00:24:43.210
out of thin air—

00:24:43.210 --> 00:24:47.150
and uses it to buy financial assets and government bonds

00:24:47.150 --> 00:24:52.260
.  This happened in the United States during the Great Depression,

00:24:52.260 --> 00:24:56.010
and again in 2008, the central bank of the United States

00:24:56.010 --> 00:24:59.940
– the Federal Reserve – printed over $2 trillion.

00:24:59.940 --> 00:25:04.179
Other central banks around the world have also printed a lot of money.  By using

00:25:04.179 --> 00:25:07.440
this money to purchase financial assets,

00:25:07.440 --> 00:25:11.679
individuals become more creditworthy as property prices rise.

00:25:11.679 --> 00:25:15.820
However, this only helps those who own financial assets.

00:25:15.820 --> 00:25:21.840
As you understand, the central bank can print money but can only purchase financial assets.

00:25:21.840 --> 00:25:24.900
The government, on the other hand,

00:25:24.900 --> 00:25:29.580
can buy goods and services and put money in people's hands,

00:25:29.580 --> 00:25:34.520
but it cannot print money.  Therefore, to stimulate the economy, cooperation between the two is

00:25:34.520 --> 00:25:35.650
necessary.  By

00:25:35.650 --> 00:25:40.470
buying government bonds, the central bank essentially lends money to the government so that

00:25:40.470 --> 00:25:41.190


00:25:41.190 --> 00:25:44.909
it can run a deficit and increase

00:25:44.909 --> 00:25:48.650
spending on goods and services and unemployment benefits to stimulate the economy

00:25:48.650 --> 00:25:53.080
.  This increases people's income and

00:25:53.080 --> 00:25:56.240
also reduces the government's debt.  However,

00:25:56.240 --> 00:25:59.540
this will reduce the overall debt burden of the economy.

00:25:59.540 --> 00:26:05.429
This is a very risky period.  Policymakers need to balance these four trends while reducing the debt burden.

00:26:05.429 --> 00:26:06.590


00:26:06.590 --> 00:26:13.050
To maintain stability, deflationary trends

00:26:13.050 --> 00:26:14.710
must be balanced with inflationary trends.  If

00:26:14.710 --> 00:26:18.130
properly balanced, this is

00:26:18.130 --> 00:26:21.479
called debt relief.

00:26:21.479 --> 00:26:25.359
Reducing debt can be an ugly or a beautiful thing.

00:26:25.359 --> 00:26:28.839
What kind of debt reduction situation would be beneficial?

00:26:28.839 --> 00:26:33.429
While reducing debt is a difficult task, it's

00:26:33.429 --> 00:26:37.549
rewarding to navigate a difficult situation in the best way possible.  It's a better situation than

00:26:37.549 --> 00:26:42.210
debt-fueled, unbalanced, excessive economic growth

00:26:42.210 --> 00:26:45.940
.  As we get rid of debt nicely,

00:26:45.940 --> 00:26:51.089
debt falls more than income, real economic growth is positive,

00:26:51.089 --> 00:26:56.889
and inflation is not a problem.  These are achieved by maintaining balance.  A

00:26:56.889 --> 00:27:00.159
state of equilibrium requires a specific mix of measures:

00:27:00.159 --> 00:27:04.229
cutting spending, reducing debt, transferring wealth,

00:27:04.229 --> 00:27:09.149
and printing money.  In this way, economic and social stability can be maintained.

00:27:09.149 --> 00:27:13.639
People wonder if inflation will rise if we print money.

00:27:13.639 --> 00:27:18.879
If it's balanced by reduced credit, then inflation won't rise.  Remember, the expenses are what matter.

00:27:18.879 --> 00:27:24.249
A dollar spent in cash

00:27:24.249 --> 00:27:25.969
has the same effect on the price as a dollar spent as credit.  The

00:27:25.969 --> 00:27:31.299
central bank prints money;   The loss of credit

00:27:31.299 --> 00:27:33.080
can be compensated for by an increase in the money supply.

00:27:33.080 --> 00:27:38.509
In order to turn things around, the central bank needs to not only pump in income growth, but

00:27:38.509 --> 00:27:39.519


00:27:39.519 --> 00:27:43.849
also generate an income growth rate greater than the interest rate on accumulated debt

00:27:43.849 --> 00:27:45.099
.

00:27:45.099 --> 00:27:48.379
What do I mean?  Essentially,

00:27:48.379 --> 00:27:52.309
revenues need to grow faster than debt.  For example, let's assume

00:27:52.309 --> 00:27:56.080
a country with a debt-to-income ratio of 100% is in a debt reduction phase

00:27:56.080 --> 00:27:58.580
.

00:27:58.580 --> 00:28:03.659
This debt amount means it is equal to the entire country's annual income

00:28:03.659 --> 00:28:05.799
.

00:28:05.799 --> 00:28:09.009
Now let's think about the interest rate on this debt.

00:28:09.009 --> 00:28:11.159
Let's take the interest rate as 2%.  If

00:28:11.159 --> 00:28:15.029
debt grows by 2% due to the interest rate, but

00:28:15.029 --> 00:28:15.690
income

00:28:15.690 --> 00:28:20.230
grows by only 1%;  This will never reduce the debt burden.  You need to print

00:28:20.230 --> 00:28:24.809
enough money to ensure that income growth exceeds the interest rate

00:28:24.809 --> 00:28:25.950
.

00:28:25.950 --> 00:28:30.720
However, printing money is very easy and can be easily abused,

00:28:30.720 --> 00:28:33.049
and people prefer printing money to alternatives.  The key is to avoid

00:28:33.049 --> 00:28:36.200
printing too much money

00:28:36.200 --> 00:28:41.110
and causing unacceptably high inflation.  Germany

00:28:41.110 --> 00:28:43.129
experienced this in the 1920s.  If

00:28:43.129 --> 00:28:48.269
policymakers strike the right balance, the debt reduction situation is not so dramatic.

00:28:48.269 --> 00:28:51.340
Growth slows, but the debt burden decreases.

00:28:51.340 --> 00:28:54.470
This is a positive step towards reducing debt.  As

00:28:54.470 --> 00:28:59.830
incomes begin to rise, borrowers also start to seem more worthy of lending.

00:28:59.830 --> 00:29:02.999
And when borrowers appear more worthy of being loaned,

00:29:02.999 --> 00:29:08.509
lenders begin to lend money.  The debt burden will eventually begin to decrease.

00:29:08.509 --> 00:29:13.499
Individuals who can borrow money spend more.  Eventually, the economy

00:29:13.499 --> 00:29:14.399
starts growing again.

00:29:14.399 --> 00:29:18.009
It enters the reflation phase from the long-term debt cycle.  If

00:29:18.009 --> 00:29:22.490
debt reduction is mismanaged, the consequences can be disastrous.

00:29:22.490 --> 00:29:26.279
If managed well, the problem will eventually be solved.

00:29:26.279 --> 00:29:29.580
Roughly speaking, over a decade or more, the

00:29:29.580 --> 00:29:33.669
debt burden decreases and economic activity normalizes

00:29:33.669 --> 00:29:37.899
– this period is called the 'lost years'.

00:29:37.899 --> 00:29:42.720
Of course, the economy is much more complex than this pattern.

00:29:42.720 --> 00:29:43.639


00:29:43.639 --> 00:29:48.850
However, the long-term debt cycle, the short-term debt cycle on top of it,

00:29:48.850 --> 00:29:52.629
and then both on top of the productivity growth curve, gives

00:29:52.629 --> 00:29:55.919
us a good model of where we are and where we are going

00:29:55.919 --> 00:29:58.919
.

00:29:58.919 --> 00:30:03.019
So, in summary, there are three basic rules I want to mention:

00:30:03.019 --> 00:30:03.720


00:30:03.720 --> 00:30:08.220
First: Don't let your debt grow faster than your income,

00:30:08.220 --> 00:30:11.429
because eventually you'll be overwhelmed by debt.

00:30:11.429 --> 00:30:16.059
Secondly: Don't let your revenue grow faster than your productivity,

00:30:16.059 --> 00:30:19.159
because ultimately, you won't be able to compete with that.

00:30:19.159 --> 00:30:24.000
And third: do everything you can to increase your production,

00:30:24.000 --> 00:30:29.029
because that's what matters most in the long run.

00:30:29.549 --> 00:30:34.080
These are simple recommendations for you and policymakers.

00:30:34.080 --> 00:30:38.470
You might be surprised, but most people – including most policymakers –

00:30:38.470 --> 00:30:39.509
don't give them enough importance.

00:30:39.509 --> 00:30:44.059
This model worked for me, and I hope it will work for you too.

00:30:44.059 --> 00:30:46.380
Thanks.

